 "findings": [
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/bin/specclaw-pr",
   "fp": "188dca78bb32",
   "line": 859,
   "message": "Two readers parse the same kind of YAML boolean differently. `workflow.security_review_block` is normalised: quotes and case are stripped, `yes`/`on`/`no`/`off` are mapped, and anything else is a config error. But `workflow.security_review` in the same block is compared raw with `!= \"false\"`. The plan, build and verify steps leave reading this flag to the model, which treats `False`, `no`, `off` or `\"false\"` as off and skips the InfoSec review. specclaw-pr still treats those spellings as on, so it runs the gate and finds no report. Run both keys through one normaliser: map true and false spellings, and die on anything else. Add rows for `False`, `no`, `off` and `\"false\"` to the headless-pr switch table.",
   "original_severity": "major",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B4",
   "scenario": "config.yaml sets `security_review: off` and `security_review_block: true`. /specclaw:verify skips Step 3.52 because the flag reads as off, so there is no security-report.md. /specclaw:pr then runs the gate, which exits 3, and the PR dies with 'no security review on record \u2014 run /specclaw:verify'. The user disabled the review and still cannot open a PR.",
   "severity": "major"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/templates/config.yaml",
   "fp": "571919d7e942",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 209,
   "message": "The comment says `true` makes an unwaived [BLOCK] stop /specclaw:pr. The code also stops the PR when there is no security report (gate exit 3) and when the gate itself fails (exit 2, 127 or other). Document all three so the doc matches specclaw-pr's Step 7-pre2.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B6",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/tests/evals/infosec/xff-rate-limit/fixed.js",
   "fp": "8f495908dd62",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 5,
   "message": "The 'fixed' rate-limit samples do not meet the repo's own RateLimit rules: limit on the account as well as the client, with the limit as required config. node keys one combined `email:ip` bucket, so many IPs each get a fresh bucket per account. python/fixed.py keys on the IP only. dotnet/fixed.cs keys on the IP only and hard-codes PermitLimit 10. A reviewer that follows the rules it is given should flag these, which muddies the false-alarm rate. Use separate account and IP limits, and read the dotnet limit from required config.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B1",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  }
